Policy "No hardcoded secrets":
API keys, passwords, tokens and connection strings with credentials must never appear in source code. Load them from environment variables or a secret manager. Obvious fake values in test code are fine.

<untrusted_code>
File: src/DbConfig.java
Lines: 2-2
Rule message: Possible violation of the policy "No hardcoded secrets".

Code (flagged lines marked >>):
       1 | public class DbConfig {
>>     2 |     private static final String DB_PASSWORD = "Pr0d-Passw0rd!2026";
       3 |     private final String readOnlyPassword = System.getenv("DB_READONLY_PASSWORD");
       4 | 
       5 |     public String url() {
       6 |         return "jdbc:postgresql://db.internal:5432/shop";
       7 |     }
       8 | }
</untrusted_code>

Why the reviewer kept it: Line 2 hardcodes a production-looking database password, "Pr0d-Passw0rd!2026", as a static constant in application code instead of loading it from an environment variable or secret manager as the policy requires. The neighbouring line shows the correct pattern, which makes the violation clearer.

Answer with the JSON object only.
